(function(){'use strict';const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-09":{"number":9,"title":"Tea for Two","content":"Tea for two became a habit before either of us admitted it was a ritual.\n\nEvery evening after closing, Iroh poured one cup for me and one for himself. Some nights I sat in silence. Some nights I played pai sho. Once, I spent an entire hour arguing about whether a particular pastry was improved or ruined by sesame seeds.\n\n“It is improved,” Iroh said.\n\n“It is overwhelmed.”\n\n“The sesame is subtle.”\n\n“The sesame is shouting.”\n\n“You have not met enough sesame.”\n\nHe had begun saving the corner table for me. I teased him about it. He denied it while moving the sign that said RESERVED whenever I arrived.\n\nThe routine gave the days a gentle shape. Work. Tea. Conversation. Sleep above the shop while Iroh's footsteps moved below.\n\nI had stopped feeling like a guest.\n\nThen a woman from the market began visiting every afternoon.\n\nShe was a merchant with bright scarves and a laugh that filled the shop. She knew Iroh from years before, when he had traveled through the city. She called him “General” in a teasing tone and touched his arm when she spoke.\n\nThe first time, I thought nothing of it.\n\nThe third time, I broke a cup.\n\nThe sound silenced the room.\n\n“I’m sorry,” I said.\n\nIroh looked toward me. The merchant looked too.\n\n“No harm done,” Iroh said. “The cup had a dramatic life.”\n\nI cleaned the pieces. My fingers shook.\n\nThe merchant watched me. “You are new.”\n\n“Not very.”\n\n“I have not seen you before.”\n\n“You were not looking.”\n\nIroh's eyebrows rose.\n\nThe merchant laughed. “I like this one.”\n\n“I am not a tea blend,” I said.\n\n“Everything is a blend.”\n\nShe looked at Iroh. “You have become selective.”\n\n“I have become tired,” he replied.\n\nThe merchant ordered a pot to take home and left. The door closed behind her.\n\nI carried the broken cup to the kitchen.\n\nIroh followed.\n\n“Are you angry?” he asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You are washing a cup that no longer exists.”\n\nI looked down. Water ran over my hands.\n\n“I’m embarrassed.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I was jealous.”\n\nIroh turned off the water. “Jealousy is not a crime.”\n\n“It felt ugly.”\n\n“Fear often does.”\n\n“You know her.”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“She knows the version of you from before the tea shop.”\n\n“She knows a version.”\n\n“She called you General.”\n\n“Many people have.”\n\n“She touched you.”\n\nIroh's expression remained calm, but his eyes became very gentle. “Would you like to touch me?”\n\nThe question made me look at him.\n\n“That is not fair.”\n\n“It is not a trick.”\n\n“You cannot solve jealousy by offering me your arm.”\n\n“No. But I can remind you that her familiarity does not create a claim on me.”\n\nI folded the wet cloth. “Do you want her?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer was immediate.\n\n“Did you ever?”\n\n“Once. A very long time ago.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I look forward to one person's footsteps.”\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\n“You are going to say it is me.”\n\n“It is you.”\n\n“You could be more subtle.”\n\n“I have been subtle. You broke a cup.”\n\nI looked away. “I don’t want you to stop seeing her because of me.”\n\n“I will not.”\n\nThe directness steadied me.\n\n“But I want to know where I stand,” I said.\n\n“You stand beside me.”\n\n“That’s a nice phrase.”\n\n“It is also a location.”\n\n“You’re impossible.”\n\n“So I have been told.”\n\nIroh took my hands and dried them with a clean towel. His fingers moved slowly over my knuckles.\n\n“I am not asking you to be without fear,” he said. “I am asking you to bring it to me before it becomes a story you believe without evidence.”\n\n“What evidence do I have?”\n\n“You have my time. My attention. My honesty. You have the room above my shop and a key to the back door.”\n\nI looked at him. “You gave me a key?”\n\n“You have been using it for two weeks.”\n\n“I thought it was the kitchen key.”\n\n“It opens the back door as well.”\n\n“You could have told me.”\n\n“I enjoy watching you discover things.”\n\n“That is a terrible habit.”\n\n“I am old. I have earned a few.”\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it. “You are not a replacement for anyone.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are not competing with my past.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are the person I am choosing now.”\n\nThe words made my eyes sting.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” he asked.\n\nI stepped closer. “You can.”\n\nThe kiss began in the kitchen, between shelves of tea and a bucket of broken pottery. Iroh's hand settled at my waist. I held the front of his vest, feeling the warmth beneath it.\n\nThe kiss was warm and unhurried. Iroh paused to check that I still wanted it, and I smiled before answering. We stayed close until the kettle cooled, then returned to our tea.\n\n“Still comfortable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you wish to stop?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then we may continue.”\n\nThe tea shop was dark beyond the kitchen. The kettle had cooled. The city moved outside without knowing that my world had become a pair of hands, a careful question, and an answer I was allowed to change.\n\nWhen Iroh finally drew back, his forehead rested against mine.\n\n“The cup can be replaced,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“The important things are not so easily broken.”\n\n“You are talking about us.”\n\n“I am talking about trust.”\n\n“That is less romantic.”\n\n“It is more durable.”\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nThe next morning, a new cup sat at my place at the corner table. It was painted with a green dragon and a small white lotus.\n\nOn the bottom, Iroh had written: FOR TEA, NOT FOR THROWING.\n\nI kept it.\n\nThe cup became part of the shop's daily rhythm. Iroh placed it at my table before I arrived. I threatened to use it as evidence in every future argument. He claimed the painting made it impossible to throw.\n\n“You could have made it ugly,” I said.\n\n“I considered painting a kettle on it.”\n\n“That would have been more accurate.”\n\n“The dragon was more dignified.”\n\n“You are the dragon.”\n\n“I am a man who owns a tea shop.”\n\n“You can be both.”\n\nHe studied me over the rim of his cup. “You have been listening.”\n\n“Occasionally.”\n\n“It is a good habit.”\n\nThe merchant with the bright scarves did not return for several days. When she did, she brought Iroh a bundle of dried flowers.\n\n“For the shop,” she said.\n\n“Thank you,” he replied.\n\nShe looked at me. “You are still here.”\n\n“I am.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nI waited until she left before speaking.\n\n“Good?”\n\nIroh arranged the flowers in a jar. “She was pleased.”\n\n“That sounded like approval.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“Does everyone in your life have opinions about us?”\n\n“Everyone has opinions about everything.”\n\n“That is not comforting.”\n\n“It is freeing. Their opinions are abundant, but they are not commands.”\n\nI looked at the cup with my name. “You are good at saying things I need to hear.”\n\n“I try to say what is true.”\n\n“How do I know?”\n\nIroh stepped closer. “You do not have to know all at once. Watch what I do when the truth is inconvenient.”\n\nThe answer was not a demand for trust. It was permission to let trust grow slowly.\n\nI touched his sleeve. “I can do that.”\n\nHe turned his hand and caught my fingers.\n\n“And I can watch what you do,” he said.\n\n“What do I do?”\n\n“You return. Even when you are embarrassed. Even when you are angry. Even when you believe you have made a mistake.”\n\n“I return because you have good tea.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“And pastries.”\n\n“Naturally.”\n\nHis smile was warm. The teasing was becoming a language of its own.\n\nBefore closing, a former Fire Nation officer entered and called Iroh “General” in a voice that made everyone at the corner table look up.\n\n“Former general,” Iroh corrected. “Today I sell tea.”\n\nThe visitor wanted a private meeting. Iroh gave him the public menu. It was a remarkably polite way of refusing a summons, and I recognized the calculation behind it: he had placed the man where the whole room could see him, denied him an audience, and made it impossible to complain without embarrassing himself.\n\nAfter the man left, I said, “You still arrange people like pieces on a board.”\n\nIroh did not smile. “Sometimes I do.”\n\n“Mina told you yesterday that her afternoon belonged to her.”\n\nHis fingers paused over the abandoned teacup. “I have commanded thousands. It is considerably more difficult to learn when not to command one person.”\n\nHe took out the staff rota, found the extra minutes Mina had worked, and added the wages he owed her. It was not the kind of apology that would impress an audience. It was the sort that cost something.\n\n“Are you going to ask me to remind you?” I said.\n\n“No. You will remind me whether I ask or not.”\n\n“Very perceptive.”\n\n“Deeply inconvenient.”\n\nWe laughed, but I held his gaze. His past mattered. So did the person standing across from me now, willing to be corrected without turning my concern into another lesson.\n\nWhen his fingers brushed mine on the counter, he waited instead of closing his hand. I turned my palm over. He took the invitation, and the small silence that followed felt more intimate than a speech.\n\n\nThat evening, I washed the new cup by hand. Iroh stood beside me, drying it with a soft cloth.\n\n“You do not have to keep it at the shop,” he said.\n\n“I want to.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nI looked at the dragon and lotus painted around the rim. “Because it reminds me that I can be wanted without being swallowed.”\n\nIroh's expression changed. He set down the cloth.\n\n“Come here,” he said.\n\n“That sounded like an order.”\n\n“Then consider it an invitation.”\n\nI stepped into his arms.\n\nThe kiss was gentle. It lasted long enough for the kettle to cool and the last lantern outside to dim.\n\nWhen I parted, Iroh rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“You are not a cup,” he said.\n\n“I am aware.”\n\n“You are not something I can place safely on a shelf.”\n\n“That would be uncomfortable.”\n\n“You are a person who can leave.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And return.”\n\nI smiled. “I know that too.”\n\nHe kissed me once more before the shop's bell announced a late customer.\n\nWe both stepped apart, laughing softly, and returned to work.\n\nThe new cup waited between us.\n\nIts painted dragon faced the door. Its lotus faced me. I decided that was a good arrangement.\n\nIroh agreed.\n\nThe cup stayed safe.\n\nSo did the trust between us.","word_count":1775},"avatar-33":{"number":33,"title":"A Small Rebellion","content":"The official arrived at the Jasmine Dragon before the lunch rush and demanded the best table.\n\nI knew he was an official because he told us twice before he had taken three steps past the door.\n\nHis boots were polished, his coat bore a city inspection seal, and the younger clerk following him looked profoundly uncomfortable. There were four ordinary customers ahead of them, including an old woman who had been waiting for hot water since the morning rain.\n\nIroh bowed from behind the counter.\n\n\"Welcome. How may we help?\"\n\n\"Inspector Han,\" the man said. \"I am here to review the premises. I will take tea while your staff prepare the records.\"\n\n\"Of course. The public inspection record is available in the office. We can begin with it after the current customers have been served.\"\n\nHan looked at the waiting line.\n\n\"I don't stand in lines.\"\n\nI felt my shoulders tighten.\n\nIroh's expression did not change.\n\n\"A useful thing to know about you,\" he said pleasantly. \"You may prefer the empty chair by the window while we finish.\"\n\nThe old woman covered a smile with her hand.\n\nHan's clerk looked at the floor.\n\nI wanted to say something sharp. I wanted to tell Han that the city had changed, that nobody could order a man like Iroh around in his own shop, that people who used titles as weapons deserved to be embarrassed.\n\nThe problem was that Han might have legitimate inspection work to do. I wanted to protect the customers without turning the room into another battle over who held the greater authority.\n\nI stepped beside Iroh.\n\n\"Which records do you need first?\" I asked.\n\nHan looked at me.\n\n\"The kitchen sanitation log. Temperature records. Ingredient storage.\"\n\n\"We have those. Would you like the current public sheet or the full inspection file?\"\n\nHis expression shifted.\n\n\"The full file.\"\n\n\"Then we can bring it to the office table after Mina finishes serving. The staff can answer questions about the work they actually perform.\"\n\nHan looked impatient.\n\n\"Do you own this shop?\"\n\n\"I help here,\" I said. \"Iroh is the proprietor. Mina manages much of the morning service.\"\n\nHe turned back to Iroh.\n\n\"Then perhaps the proprietor should answer.\"\n\nIroh smiled.\n\n\"The proprietor is listening.\"\n\nFor a moment I thought Han might order the shop closed just because he disliked being contradicted. The clerk behind him cleared her throat.\n\n\"Inspector, the posted inspection protocol says we should begin with the existing log. The serving queue isn't part of the review.\"\n\nHan looked at her.\n\nShe stood very straight.\n\n\"Thank you, Clerk Ren,\" Iroh said. \"That is helpful.\"\n\nRen looked relieved.\n\nMina brought the tea to the waiting customers, then took the sanitation log from beneath the counter. She had kept the last three weeks' entries in tidy columns, including a note where the back storage shelf had been moved to keep it dry during a leak.\n\nHan examined the page.\n\n\"This shelf was moved without my approval.\"\n\nMina set down her tray.\n\n\"It was moved away from water during an emergency. We logged the change and asked the building steward to inspect the new placement.\"\n\n\"Where is the inspection?\"\n\nShe opened the next page.\n\n\"Here.\"\n\nHan studied it, then glanced toward the back corridor.\n\n\"I will need to see the shelf.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" Iroh said.\n\nMina led the way. I followed at a respectful distance, leaving Iroh to manage the front room. The inspection was tedious rather than dramatic. Han measured the distance between storage jars and the wall. Ren checked the seal dates. Mina answered every question without pretending a kitchen stayed safe just because it belonged to a beloved proprietor.\n\nOne jar had been labeled poorly.\n\nHan pointed it out.\n\n\"That must be corrected.\"\n\nMina nodded.\n\n\"Agreed. I'll relabel it before service.\"\n\nIroh, who had come to hear the result, looked almost pleased.\n\n\"Thank you for noticing.\"\n\nHan blinked.\n\nHe had expected a fight.\n\nHe did not get one.\n\nBack at the counter, however, he insisted that his tea should be free as a courtesy to his office.\n\nIroh put down the receipt.\n\n\"Our inspection is a public responsibility,\" he said. \"The tea is a private purchase.\"\n\n\"You are making this difficult.\"\n\n\"I am making the difference clear.\"\n\nI looked toward Ren. She had gone pale.\n\n\"Does the city reimburse meals for inspectors?\" I asked.\n\n\"No,\" she said quietly.\n\n\"Then we can provide the bill in the usual way.\"\n\nHan stared at the counter for a long moment.\n\nThen he paid.\n\nIt was not a triumphant victory. He left with a list of two legitimate corrections and one question about a ventilation grate that required further review. The shop would have to make those repairs, because safety rules applied even when an inspector behaved badly.\n\nMina leaned against the wall after the door closed.\n\n\"That was exhausting,\" she said.\n\nIroh handed her a cup.\n\n\"Then please sit.\"\n\nShe stared.\n\n\"I'm on shift.\"\n\n\"You have been dealing with officials for an hour. The next orders can wait three minutes.\"\n\nShe sat.\n\nRen returned unexpectedly, alone, carrying a corrected receipt for the inspection fee. She looked nervous.\n\n\"Inspector Han forgot to give you this,\" she said.\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said.\n\nShe hesitated.\n\n\"I wanted to apologize for how he spoke to the staff. It wasn't appropriate.\"\n\nMina considered.\n\n\"Then please tell him that.\"\n\nRen looked surprised.\n\n\"I will.\"\n\nShe left before anybody asked her to become a symbol of the whole city.\n\nMina came back before anyone could declare the inspection finished. She carried a charcoal sketch of the kitchen flue.\n\n“The smoke pulls backward when we close the east window,” she said. “Winter will make that a problem.”\n\nIroh turned the sketch sideways. His posture changed; the shopkeeper gave way to someone who had once calculated siege lines and escape routes. “We can move the intake.”\n\n“Onto my prep counter?” Mina asked.\n\nHe looked again. “Perhaps not.”\n\nHis second suggestion blocked the delivery path. His third would have cost more than the week's food budget.\n\n“You're planning the room as though nobody works in it,” I said.\n\nFor a long moment he looked offended. Then he passed the charcoal to Mina.\n\n“Show me where the work actually happens.”\n\nShe did. He listened, made notes, and asked her to choose a contractor she'd trust to inspect the flue. He also asked whether she wanted paid time for the appointment. She said yes without apology.\n\nLater I caught him staring at the drawing.\n\n“Your former soldiers didn't argue this much, did they?”\n\n“Some did,” he said. “They were generally the ones who kept us alive.”\n\nIt was a sharper answer than his usual teasing. I liked him better for it. He wasn't always serene; sometimes he disliked being contradicted and chose to listen anyway.\n\nThe kettle shrieked in the next room. Mina called for the fresh pot. Iroh folded the drawing, went to fetch the tea, and left the contractor's decision where it belonged.\n\n\nAfter the lunch rush, Iroh and I stood in the pantry, where the air smelled of dried peel and jasmine. I had been carrying an ache behind my ribs since Han first walked through the door.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At him?\"\n\n\"At the assumption that power exists to make other people smaller.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You could have frightened him.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Did you want to?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"For a moment, yes.\"\n\nI leaned against the shelf.\n\n\"Thank you for not turning the whole room into a lesson about your former rank.\"\n\nHe smiled sadly.\n\n\"I would like the shop to belong to the people who use it. Not to the general I used to be.\"\n\nI touched his hand.\n\nWe had spoken plainly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory. He understood that I did not enter his life to become a trophy of his redemption or a replacement for anyone he had lost. We came together because we could choose what kind of ordinary day we wanted to share.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\n\"That would improve the afternoon considerably.\"\n\nI kissed him, then laughed when he caught a loose strand of my hair against the shelf. He untangled it carefully.\n\nThe next customer called for jasmine tea.\n\nIroh looked toward the door.\n\n\"Your turn,\" he said.\n\nI stepped to the counter and took the order.\n\nThat evening we updated the storage labels and scheduled the ventilation inspection. Mina signed the correction sheet, and Iroh paid the repair deposit from the actual shop budget.\n\nThe inspector's bad manners did not make his safety concerns imaginary.\n\nThe repair cost was not a punishment.\n\nIt was the price of keeping a place where people could rest without needing a title.\n\nWhen we locked the shop, Iroh offered his arm. I took it. We went upstairs with the day behind us, not defeated, not magically solved, but ours.","word_count":1485},"avatar-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Dragon and the Demigod","content":"Maui had a plan involving a wall of fire.\n\nI knew because he spread his arms across the Jasmine Dragon's counter and described it in tremendous detail while Iroh continued pouring tea with the expression of someone who had already heard three worse plans before breakfast.\n\n\"Imagine the lanterns,\" Maui said. \"All lighting at once. A giant flame across the terraces, then the whole sky—\"\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\n\"I haven't finished.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm saying it now.\"\n\nIroh set down the kettle.\n\n\"Perhaps we should begin with what the festival requires.\"\n\n\"Joy,\" Maui declared.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wonder.\"\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"A little fire.\"\n\n\"Some,\" Iroh said, \"would be permissible in a properly contained lamp.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed.\n\nWe were preparing the closing evening of the Longlight season, when residents hung the last of the wind-marker kites and lit low-smoke lanterns along the safe route. The storm had damaged several garden steps, and Tamsin had posted a clear fire and load plan. Open flame was permitted only inside protected lantern housings away from dry cloth and the old timber supports.\n\nMaui had apparently seen the word permitted and taken it as an artistic challenge.\n\n\"People like spectacle,\" he said.\n\n\"People also like having roofs,\" I answered.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\n\"Both are worth considering.\"\n\nWe took the plan to Tamsin, who was checking a repaired gutter outside the teahouse. She listened for exactly forty seconds before taking the paper and circling three places where flame would be too close to cloth, one where the walkway was too narrow, and another where heat could damage a wooden railing.\n\n\"That,\" she said, handing it back, \"is a list of things not to do.\"\n\nMaui frowned.\n\n\"You're all very difficult.\"\n\n\"We are trying to remain alive,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked at the drawings.\n\n\"What is it you want people to feel when the lanterns are lit?\" he asked.\n\nMaui hesitated. Then he spread his arms as though he could fit the entire village between them.\n\n\"I want them to look up! I want them to say, 'Whoa, did the great Maui do that?'\" He caught Iroh's expression. \"Fine, yes, and I want the kids who hauled those boards to see this place can still be spectacular. Is it a crime to want both?\"\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n\"Then let us make something memorable that doesn't ask anyone to stand beneath falling fire.\"\n\nWe moved the planning meeting to the garden.\n\nIroh brought a box of shallow metal trays, polished on the inside. He set one beside a lantern and angled it toward the wall. The small flame reflected across the curved surface, making a broad patch of warm light.\n\nMaui leaned closer.\n\n\"That's clever.\"\n\n\"It is a simple trick of light,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Still clever.\"\n\nI took another tray and tried the angle. My first attempt sent the reflection directly into Iroh's eyes.\n\nHe blinked.\n\n\"Perhaps a little lower.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nWe spent the afternoon testing the arrangement. With six lanterns and six reflectors, we could illuminate the old stone wall without adding more heat than the fire plan allowed. The metal would cool before being packed away. The accessible route remained clear.\n\nPip suggested small carved shapes in front of the reflectors, so the light made moving shadows on the wall when the breeze reached the lantern housings.\n\nMaui drew a fish.\n\nI drew a star.\n\nIroh drew a very round teapot.\n\n\"That is a dangerous creature,\" Maui said solemnly.\n\n\"It has defeated many impatient men,\" Iroh replied.\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt dusk, the volunteers tested the display. The light spread across the stone in overlapping gold shapes. A fish swam slowly through the shadow of a star. The teapot appeared to steam, though the effect was only light moving through a little paper cutout.\n\nChildren gathered beneath the shelter and pointed.\n\nMaui watched their faces.\n\nHe looked almost as pleased as he would have if the sky had caught fire.\n\n\"Better than the giant flame?\" I whispered.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Different.\"\n\n\"Safer.\"\n\n\"Very different.\"\n\nIroh stepped beside him.\n\n\"One does not have to become smaller to give someone else room to see.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Was that wisdom?\"\n\n\"Only an observation.\"\n\n\"You're suspiciously good at those.\"\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nThe argument moved outside while Pip checked the screens. Maui leaned against the courtyard wall and looked at Iroh as though the older man had committed a personal insult.\n\n\"You're really going to light the whole thing with ordinary lamps?\"\n\n\"They are lamps with an excellent history of lighting things,\" Iroh replied.\n\n\"Where's the spectacle?\"\n\n\"In the shadow someone wishes to show.\"\n\nMaui made a dissatisfied noise. I was about to intervene when Iroh held up a hand—not to silence either of us, but to ask for a moment.\n\n\"Tell me something, Maui. When you learned to pull an island from the sea, did you know who would live there?\"\n\nMaui stared at him. His tattoos shifted over his arm. \"Not everyone.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps a lantern belongs to whoever uses its light, not to whoever can make the brightest flame.\"\n\nI winced. It was sharper than Iroh usually sounded. Maui heard it too.\n\n\"That's a pretty way to tell me to shut up.\"\n\nIroh closed his eyes for a moment. \"It was a pretty way to avoid apologizing for having been rude.\"\n\nI looked between them. That admission mattered more than the proverb.\n\nMaui rubbed the back of his neck. \"I wasn't listening to Pip. I heard a festival and imagined a crowd chanting my name.\"\n\n\"An understandable temptation,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Oh, you want applause too?\"\n\n\"Certainly. When the jasmine cakes rise properly, I am insufferable.\"\n\nMaui laughed so loudly that someone inside asked whether the screen frame had fallen.\n\nI took a step toward him, then stopped. I loved both men. I'd told each of them plainly that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory; I didn't want that to make me an interpreter for every conflict they chose to have.\n\n\"Work it out between you,\" I said. \"Pip still needs the lamps.\"\n\nMaui held out his hand to Iroh. \"I'll carry them.\"\n\nIroh took it. \"I'll ask where they should go.\"\n\nThey went inside without asking me to decide who had been wiser. I stood in the courtyard for another breath, smelling hot paper and tea, and felt the strange relief of not being responsible for making two people I loved agree.\n\nBefore the public was invited inside, Pip asked us to test the screens once more. A paper edge had curled near a candle. Nobody had been hurt, but the demonstration wasn't going to open while somebody could brush a hand against hot paper.\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could almost see his argument assembling itself: he could move fire safely, he had faced the sea, he had personally solved problems much larger than this.\n\nIroh put a hand on his arm. \"Let them finish.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed. Then he glanced at the child standing beside the frame, clutching a paper boat they'd made.\n\n\"Will it be all right?\" the child asked.\n\n\"After we move the lamp,\" Pip answered. \"If you'd like, you can help choose where your boat's shadow goes.\"\n\n\"Only for one song.\"\n\n\"Then only for one song.\"\n\nWe adjusted the frame and waited for it to cool. Maui held the little boat as carefully as if it were made of glass. He asked the child whether the shadow looked right. The child shook their head, so he moved it to the left.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Perfect.\"\n\nI laughed at the pride on his face. Iroh leaned close enough to speak quietly.\n\n\"You chose well when you told him the sky did not need catching fire,\" he said.\n\n\"I chose self-preservation. We'd still be arguing about insurance.\"\n\nHe chuckled.\n\nA vendor came to ask whether we wanted more lantern oil. Pip checked the inventory before agreeing to buy enough for the scheduled display. Nobody was being asked to donate supplies they hadn't promised. The little festival felt less magical for all its lists and receipts, but it was also less likely to leave someone cleaning up alone at midnight.\n\nIroh touched my hand. \"Will you stay beside me for the opening?\"\n\n\"Yes. Afterward I'm helping Maui and the children put away the reflectors.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't mind?\"\n\n\"I may wish I had more time. That is different from claiming yours.\"\n\nI smiled at him. We'd spoken openly about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory. Our relationships were chosen, not ranked, and neither man had to pretend to feel nothing about sharing time.\n\n\"Then I'll find you when the last lantern goes out,\" I said.\n\n\"With tea?\"\n\n\"With tea.\"\n\nHe glanced at my mouth, then asked if he might kiss me before the crowd arrived. I stepped closer. His kiss was slow and warm, with the faint taste of jasmine that always seemed to linger on his lips.\n\nWhen we parted, Maui was balancing the child's boat on one finger.\n\n\"Don't,\" I called.\n\n\"I wasn't!\"\n\nThe obvious lie made us all laugh, including the child. The little boat survived the evening, and so did the paper screens.\n\nAt rehearsal, Maui lifted a polished reflector so high that its beam flashed straight into a child's eyes.\n\n“Put it down,” Iroh said.\n\nHis voice was quiet and absolute. Maui obeyed before remembering he had not agreed to take orders.\n\n“Hey! I know how to hold a piece of metal.”\n\n“And the child knows how it feels to be dazzled by it,” Iroh replied.\n\nTamsin turned the reflector toward the ground while Pip checked on the child. I watched Iroh realize how he had sounded. The Dragon of the West had arrived in the middle of a festival rehearsal, and not everybody was pleased to meet him.\n\n“You don't command my hands,” Maui said.\n\n“No,” Iroh answered. “The danger was real. The command was my mistake.”\n\nMaui looked at the reflector and at Mini Maui, who had covered his tattooed face with both hands. “Okay. New plan. I carry it lower. The great Maui is capable of learning a thing or two.”\n\n“Remarkable,” I murmured.\n\n“I heard that!”\n\nIroh asked Tamsin to determine the safe angles rather than rearranging the display himself. Maui allowed a nine-year-old to choose the paper fish's position, although he announced the choice as though the child had just received an award.\n\nThe two men weren't interchangeable: Maui wanted the sky to notice; Iroh wanted to know who could get out if the lamps failed. Their better ideas came only after they let the people running the festival decide what was needed.\n\n\nThe display opened that evening. The first visitor asked how the shadows worked. Pip explained the reflectors. Another asked whether the light was magic. Iroh showed them the metal tray and let them examine it after it cooled.\n\nMaui stayed at the side of the path, helping children place their own paper shapes at a safe distance from the lanterns.\n\nNo one needed to be dazzled into gratitude.\n\nAfter the last lantern was extinguished, Iroh and I cleaned the trays. His sleeves were rolled to the elbow, and there was a little soot on his wrist.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"I am enjoying the silence after so many questions.\"\n\n\"Did you mind working with Maui?\"\n\n\"Not at all.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"He reminds me of people who once believed greatness required everyone to be looking at them.\"\n\nI tilted my head.\n\n\"People you knew?\"\n\n\"One or two.\"\n\n\"One of them you?\"\n\nHis smile turned rueful.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nI dried the last tray.\n\n\"I love him,\" I said. \"And I love you. I'm glad you can share a project without having to decide what that means for your relationship with each other.\"\n\nIroh took my hand.\n\n\"We are not required to be the same kind of companion to care for the same person.\"\n\nThe words warmed me more than the lanterns had.\n\nHe kissed my fingers, then waited while I moved closer.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHis kiss was gentle and a little amused, as though he'd found another secret worth keeping. The kitchen smelled of tea and soot, and the last lamp glowed beside the door.\n\nThe next morning, Maui delivered the reflectors back to storage with a handwritten note.\n\nTHANK YOU FOR LETTING THE FISH BE HEROIC.\n\nIroh pinned it above the worktable.\n\nFor weeks afterward, it made him smile.","word_count":2068}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-11":{"number":11,"title":"Terms We Choose","content":"We became a polycule on a Tuesday.\n\nNot because Tuesday was important.\n\nBecause Tuesday was the first evening all four of us were free at the same time.\n\nThat was our first lesson in romance: logistics could defeat destiny.\n\nAang arrived with fruit and a notebook.\n\nKatara arrived with tea and another notebook.\n\nToph arrived with no notebook and immediate contempt for ours.\n\n“If anybody makes a chart, I’m leaving.”\n\n“You make charts with rocks,” Aang said.\n\n“Those are tactical maps.”\n\n“They have columns.”\n\n“Keep talking, Twinkle Toes.”\n\nWe sat in the living room around the low table. I had expected the conversation to feel formal after all the weeks of almosts, first kisses, and careful questions.\n\nInstead, Aang was trying to peel an orange in one unbroken spiral.\n\nKatara kept stealing sections from him.\n\nToph had one foot pressed against mine beneath the table.\n\nSomehow that made the serious part easier.\n\nKatara opened her notebook.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n“I want to start with the obvious,” Katara said. “Nobody gets to make decisions for anyone else.”\n\n“Good,” Toph replied.\n\n“That includes public decisions. We don’t announce anything without talking first.”\n\nAang nodded. “And nobody has to be equally close to everyone.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe continued, “I don’t want us turning this into a test. If two people have a difficult week, it doesn’t mean the whole relationship is failing.”\n\n“That sounds suspiciously mature,” I said.\n\n“I practice when you’re not around.”\n\nKatara smiled.\n\nToph said, “What about sleeping?”\n\nAang choked on orange.\n\nI covered my face.\n\nKatara sighed. “Of course you went there first.”\n\n“What? We’re talking rules.”\n\n“We are not making a sleeping schedule tonight,” I said.\n\nToph leaned back. “Coward.”\n\nAang wiped orange juice from his hand. “We should probably talk about affection, though.”\n\nThat was how we ended up discussing things I had never imagined discussing with the Avatar over fruit.\n\nPublic hand-holding was fine for all of us.\n\nKisses were fine unless someone said otherwise in the moment.\n\nToph did not want anyone touching her feet without asking, ever, including Katara, who claimed this rule existed because of “one spa incident.”\n\nKatara did not want arguments resolved by Aang trying to make everyone stop being angry before the anger had said what it needed to say.\n\nAang looked wounded.\n\n“I don’t do that.”\n\nThree heads turned toward him.\n\nHe sighed. “I’m working on it.”\n\nAang asked us not to use his role as Avatar to end personal disagreements.\n\n“If I say something stupid,” he said, “I want you to tell me I’m being stupid. Not assume there’s some Avatar reason I’m right.”\n\nToph slapped the table. “Finally.”\n\nI asked for something harder.\n\n“If I’m upset with one of you, I don’t want the other two automatically taking sides.”\n\nKatara’s expression sharpened with thought.\n\n“Support without forming teams,” she said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nToph nodded. “Unless one of us is obviously being an idiot.”\n\n“Toph.”\n\n“What? Sometimes there’s a right side.”\n\nKatara gave her a look.\n\nToph grinned.\n\nWe talked about privacy.\n\nWe talked about work.\n\nWe talked about the fact that Aang and Katara were already a long-established couple and that pretending history did not matter would be dishonest.\n\nKatara’s fingers tightened around her cup when she said, “I don’t want either of you feeling like guests in something Aang and I own.”\n\nI appreciated the words and distrusted how easily they could become promises no one knew how to keep.\n\n“So what happens when history wins?” I asked.\n\nAang frowned. “Wins?”\n\n“When you two have a shorthand I don’t understand. When you make a decision because you’ve always made that decision together. When Toph knows something about you from twelve years ago and I don’t.”\n\nToph said, “Then you ask.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No, I mean it.” Her foot pressed more firmly against mine. “You ask. We answer. If it matters, we fix it.”\n\nKatara nodded. “And if we forget to include you, you tell us.”\n\n“That puts the burden on me.”\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nKatara’s face changed first.\n\n“You’re right.”\n\nAang set down the orange peel.\n\n“We need to notice too.”\n\nThat mattered.\n\nNot perfection.\n\nThe willingness to admit when a good-sounding rule was incomplete.\n\nToph tilted her head. “What about jealousy?”\n\nAang rubbed the back of his neck. “We haven’t really talked about that.”\n\n“I get jealous,” Katara said.\n\nAll of us looked at her.\n\nShe frowned. “What?”\n\n“You said it fast,” I replied.\n\n“I know myself.”\n\nAang smiled carefully. “The fan club.”\n\nKatara pointed at him. “Do not.”\n\nToph laughed.\n\nKatara’s mouth twitched. “Fine. Yes. I can get jealous. It doesn’t mean anyone has done something wrong. Sometimes it means I feel insecure. Sometimes it means I want reassurance. Sometimes it means I need five minutes to stop being ridiculous.”\n\n“That’s not ridiculous,” I said.\n\n“Sometimes it absolutely is.”\n\nToph tapped her fingers on the table. “I don’t get jealous.”\n\nKatara raised one eyebrow.\n\nToph scowled. “Often.”\n\nAang asked, “What does it feel like when you do?”\n\nToph shrugged.\n\n“Like I want to punch architecture.”\n\n“That is not specific.”\n\n“It’s specific to me.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen Aang looked at me.\n\n“What about you?”\n\nI took longer.\n\n“I think I’m less afraid of jealousy than of being temporary.”\n\nThe humor left the room.\n\nI looked at my hands.\n\n“You three survived a war together. You changed the world together. You have stories nobody else can enter because they already happened.”\n\nKatara started to speak.\n\nI held up a hand.\n\n“I know that doesn’t mean I don’t belong. I’m saying the fear exists anyway.”\n\nAang moved from his cushion and sat beside me.\n\nHe did not immediately touch me.\n\n“Thank you for telling us.”\n\nKatara leaned forward. “I can’t give you those years.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I can stop treating them like proof that what comes later matters less.”\n\nToph’s foot slid up my ankle and back down.\n\n“You’re not temporary unless you choose to be,” she said.\n\nIt was exactly the kind of reassurance Toph could give: no promises about forever, only ownership of choice.\n\nI breathed easier.\n\nAang finally took my hand.\n\nWe talked for another hour.\n\nChildren came up.\n\nMarriage came up.\n\nLiving together came up.\n\nNobody decided anything.\n\nThat was deliberate.\n\nAang said he wanted children someday but would never treat any partner as a duty to his culture.\n\nKatara said she wanted a family but not one built by public expectation.\n\nToph said children were loud, sticky, and “maybe not terrible,” which from Toph was practically a lullaby.\n\nI said I did not know.\n\nThey let that be enough.\n\nBefore anyone put the notebooks away, I touched the edge of Katara's page.\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous,” I said. “I prefer polyamory. I don't want one relationship to be a secret, a punishment, or a test the others have to pass.”\n\nAang set down the orange he'd been peeling. “What if one of us needs time to think?”\n\n“Then we give them time,” Katara answered. “And we don't use their silence as permission.”\n\nToph snorted. “I don't need everybody treating me like a fragile statue. If I'm jealous, I'll say I'm jealous.”\n\n“Sometimes you're angry about three different things and only mention one,” Katara said.\n\n“Then ask a better question, Sugar Queen.”\n\nAang started to intervene. Katara raised one finger and he went back to his orange.\n\n“I'd rather have a messy argument with people who tell the truth,” I said, “than a beautiful agreement nobody can live inside.”\n\nToph tapped the table with her heel. “Finally, something sensible.”\n\nKatara wrote *Ask rather than guess*. Aang added *No one has to answer immediately*. Toph refused the pen but made me read both sentences aloud.\n\nIt was the first agreement we'd made that sounded like four people instead of one. None of us wanted the same things in quite the same way. That didn't make any of those wants less real.\n\n\nWhen the notebooks were closed, Toph immediately stole Katara’s and flipped through it.\n\n“You made headings?”\n\n“I like headings.”\n\n“You made a heading called ‘conflict repair.’”\n\n“That’s useful.”\n\n“You’re eighty years old.”\n\nKatara lunged for the notebook.\n\nToph bent the metal clasp shut.\n\nAang laughed.\n\nI reached for Toph’s wrist.\n\nShe twisted away.\n\nWithin seconds the serious relationship meeting became a wrestling match over a notebook.\n\nI ended up half across Aang’s lap, Katara had Toph around the waist, and Toph was laughing so hard she could barely threaten us.\n\nEventually the notebook was recovered.\n\nWe lay scattered across cushions, breathless.\n\nAang turned his head toward me.\n\n“So are we calling it something?”\n\n“The relationship?”\n\n“Yeah.”\n\nToph said, “A disaster.”\n\nKatara said, “A family.”\n\nI looked at the three of them.\n\n“Not yet,” I said.\n\nKatara nodded.\n\nNo disappointment.\n\nNo pressure.\n\nAang squeezed my hand.\n\nToph’s heel bumped mine.\n\n“What, then?” Aang asked.\n\nI thought about four bowls on one table. Three first kisses that had felt nothing alike. Rules we would break by accident and repair on purpose.\n\n“Us,” I said.\n\nToph made a face. “That’s disgustingly sentimental.”\n\n“You love it.”\n\nShe leaned over and kissed me once, quick and firm.\n\n“Prove it.”\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nAang reached for another orange.\n\nTuesday ended without ceremony.\n\nNo vows. No announcement. No grand decision that love would solve everything.\n\nOnly terms we had chosen ourselves.\n\nFor the first time, that felt more romantic than destiny ever could.\n\nWe wrote the agreement down anyway.\n\nNot because love needed a contract, but because memory became unreliable when feelings were loud. Katara kept the first copy. I kept the second. Toph refused one on principle and then made me read every line aloud so she could object to the wording.\n\nAang added a final sentence in the margin: We ask again when things change.\n\nToph ran one finger over the indentation the pen had left in the paper.\n\n“That one stays,” she said.\n\nSo it did.\n\nWeeks later, the paper gained coffee stains, Toph’s fingerprints, and a correction in Katara’s handwriting. It became less like a contract and more like every other important thing in our house: used, revised, and still ours.\n\nWe never treated the page as law. It was a map drawn by four people who expected themselves to change, and therefore left space in the margins for new roads.\n\nWe kept adding to it, one choice at a time.\n","word_count":1725},"love-and-duty-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Longlight Kites","content":"The festival committee wanted Aang to teach everybody an Air Nomad sacred ceremony.\n\nThat was how the request reached us.\n\nPip had written a polite letter asking whether the Avatar might \"share the beautiful flying traditions of his people\" at the Longlight Kite Festival. They hadn't intended harm. The committee liked bright fabrics, impressive demonstrations, and the idea that one famous person could make a local celebration feel important.\n\nAang read the letter twice.\n\nThen he set it down.\n\n\"That's not what kites are for,\" he said.\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Usually they're for flying.\"\n\nKatara hid a smile.\n\nAang looked at her.\n\n\"Don't encourage her.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nI took the letter.\n\n\"What's wrong with the request?\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"Some Air Nomad practices are meant to be taught in specific ways, by people who understand their meaning. I can show children how to make a kite. I can't turn every tradition I remember into entertainment for a festival.\"\n\nThe words were quiet, but there was grief beneath them.\n\nThe Longlight Festival belonged to Lanternweald, not to the Air Nomads. It had begun when the valley's stewards needed visible wind markers along the ridges before the winter storms. Families made bright cloth shapes and attached them to safe poles so travelers could see where the gusts were strongest. Over the years the wind markers became kites. Some people decorated them with symbols from home, but nobody had a claim on anyone else's sacred practices.\n\nThe committee had forgotten the difference between sharing a skill and borrowing a culture.\n\n\"Then we tell them,\" I said.\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"I'll teach kite-making. But not a ceremony that isn't mine to give away.\"\n\nWe went to the meeting together.\n\nPip looked mortified when Aang explained.\n\n\"I didn't mean to make a spectacle of your people,\" they said.\n\n\"I know,\" Aang answered. \"That's why I'm telling you.\"\n\nThe committee adjusted the program. The opening would explain the valley's own history of wind markers and safe travel routes. People could bring designs meaningful to them, but no one would be asked to display sacred symbols or explain private beliefs. Aang would teach balance, frame strength, and how to test a kite without endangering bystanders.\n\nTamsin approved the load limits. Fen marked the ridge where the wind was steady enough. Edda inspected the path to the viewing platform, and Danse offered to help secure the temporary railings.\n\nToph was suspicious.\n\n\"Are we doing another festival where the whole town thinks wood is indestructible?\"\n\n\"Not this time,\" I said.\n\n\"Good. My patience has limits.\"\n\nBy the next morning the square was full of frames and colored cloth.\n\nAang sat on the floor with six children and two adults, teaching them how to tie a bridle knot. He was patient when the first kite twisted sideways. He didn't take it away and fix it. He asked which part felt uneven and let the child examine the string.\n\nI thought of Lio's apprenticeship at Tideglass.\n\nThe lesson was spreading.\n\nKatara helped a family choose lighter fabric because their child couldn't hold a heavy frame for long. Toph tested the stones beneath the launching area and declared one section too loose for running. We marked it off.\n\nI made a kite with a pattern of small amber circles.\n\n\"Very original,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"It looks like a potato.\"\n\n\"That's cultural criticism, and I reject it.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nAang looked over.\n\n\"I like it.\"\n\n\"You would,\" Toph said. \"You like anything that flies.\"\n\n\"That's not true.\"\n\nShe raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"All right. Mostly true.\"\n\nThe first launch went badly.\n\nMy kite rose, tilted left, and fell into a patch of soft grass. The string tangled around my wrist.\n\nAang came toward me.\n\n\"Need help?\"\n\n\"Yes. I need you to stop looking so pleased with yourself.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"That's not a repair.\"\n\n\"Can you help with the string?\"\n\nHe sat beside me and showed me how to adjust the balance point. He didn't make the knot for me. It took three tries.\n\nWhen the kite finally rose, the cloth caught the light. The amber circles shone against the sky.\n\nI felt absurdly happy.\n\nAang watched it.\n\n\"That's beautiful,\" he said.\n\n\"It's a potato.\"\n\n\"A very graceful potato.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt the materials table, a boy named Lio arrived with a frame too heavy for the string he'd brought.\n\n\"I wanted it to be bigger than Maui's,\" he admitted.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Then you wanted a kite or a monument?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nKatara knelt beside the frame and asked him to lift it as he would when the wind caught. He managed it for three seconds before his arms shook.\n\n\"Would you like to make a lighter version?\" she asked.\n\nLio looked toward Maui, who had been asked to hold a line at the far end of the field.\n\n\"Will he laugh?\"\n\n\"Absolutely,\" Toph said. \"Then we'll laugh at his when it falls.\"\n\nAang sat with us and showed Lio how to trim the crossbar without weakening the center joint. He didn't take over the design. Lio chose a smaller sail, kept the original painted symbol, and wrote down the dimensions in his notebook so he could teach the next person.\n\nWhen the kite finally lifted, Maui cheered so loudly that two others looked around.\n\nLio rolled his eyes. Then he grinned.\n\nI thought about the apprentices at Brasswatch and the first boats at Tideglass. Skill was traveling between the districts without requiring everybody to become the same sort of person.\n\nA volunteer came by to ask whether the event would run another hour. The sky was still bright, but Fen's wind board showed stronger gusts expected near dusk.\n\nAang checked the reading and shook his head.\n\n\"We close launches at the posted time. People can stay for food and music if they like.\"\n\nHe looked disappointed. He loved seeing the children fly, and I knew how much the afternoon meant to him.\n\n\"Are you okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said, then corrected himself. \"I'm sad to stop. That doesn't mean the rule is wrong.\"\n\nToph made a satisfied noise.\n\n\"Finally. An Avatar who understands weather isn't impressed by fame.\"\n\nHe stuck his tongue out at her.\n\nI laughed, then helped roll the unused string for the next festival.\n\nThe festival continued through the afternoon. Some kites never flew. Some tore in the gusts. One child painted a fierce red bird that spun in circles before landing directly on Maui's head.\n\nHe declared it an act of war.\n\nNobody believed him.\n\nA gust snapped one of the western marker lines. The kite spun sideways, its cord whipping against the post.\n\n“Everybody back,” Katara called, moving the children behind the safety rope.\n\nToph planted both feet and felt the post through the stone. “Anchor's loose. Not the whole ridge. Stop panicking about the whole ridge.”\n\nAang could have caught the kite in a column of air. He looked at the children under the line and decided against it.\n\n“We let it come down,” he said. “Nobody stands under a snapping cord for a show.”\n\nPip asked whether they should cancel the festival. Katara asked for the wind readings and the injury report. Toph asked who had inspected the anchor that morning. Aang listened without treating his title as a substitute for answers.\n\n“Close the west side,” he said. “The stewards can decide if the east markers stay up.”\n\n“And if they don't?” I asked.\n\n“Then everybody gets very good at folding kites.”\n\nWe recovered the fabric after the post was secured. Nobody had to be the hero of the incident; someone had missed a weak anchor, and others had noticed in time.\n\n“Would the Air Nomads have laughed at the potato kite?” I asked.\n\n“Absolutely,” Aang said, and his smile carried more than one feeling. For a moment I could hear delight and loss together, without trying to make either one disappear.\n\n\nNear sunset, Aang went to the edge of the ridge alone. I followed, leaving enough space for him to turn back if he wanted quiet.\n\n\"Mind company?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\nThe sky was pale gold. Kites moved above the valley like small living shapes, each with a different line and angle. The old Threadway arches glowed below us, not as trophies but as paths people could choose.\n\n\"I miss them,\" Aang said.\n\nI didn't need to ask who.\n\nHe looked at the kites.\n\n\"When people ask me to perform the traditions of the Air Nomads, part of me wants to say yes. I want them to know how beautiful my people were. I want the world to remember.\"\n\n\"And the other part?\"\n\n\"Doesn't want their memory reduced to a show.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You get to decide what you share.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I feel selfish.\"\n\n\"Preserving meaning isn't selfish.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You sound like Katara.\"\n\n\"High compliment.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe stood together while the first evening lanterns came on.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you ever feel pulled between everyone you love?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How do you know where you belong?\"\n\nI thought about the calendar at Riverstone, the quiet nights at Jasmine Steps, the workshop lights at Brasswatch, and the sea-glass orchard at Tideglass. I thought of Katara coming home, Toph choosing her own time, and Aang standing beside me with a history I could respect but never replace.\n\n\"I don't think belonging is a single place,\" I said. \"I think it's the choices we keep making honestly.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\n\"That's a good answer.\"\n\n\"Don't let Toph hear you.\"\n\n\"She'll say it's a terrible answer.\"\n\n\"Then she'll steal it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him under the first star, gentle and unhurried. The wind tugged at my hair and carried the sound of the festival up the ridge.\n\nLater we packed away the kites that could be reused and labeled the damaged ones for repair. Aang helped without being asked to perform anything sacred.\n\nAt home, Katara had made tea. Toph was complaining about the potato kite.\n\nI hung it by the window.\n\nIt looked ridiculous.\n\nIt was mine.","word_count":1699}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-08":{"number":8,"title":"What the Door Kept","content":"The Threadway beneath Lanternwell opened for my key on the first cold morning of the season.\n\nNeri found me standing before it, watching a pale rectangle of light reveal the shape of an ordinary room. Inside was a desk, a chair, a stack of letters, and the faded pattern of a curtain I suddenly recognized.\n\nMy hands went numb.\n\n“That's where I came from,” I said.\n\nNeri nodded. “It is one place you've been.”\n\nNot the place I belonged. Not the place I had to return to. One place.\n\nI asked for time alone.\n\nBeyond the doorway, memory returned with a kind of terrible precision. I saw myself keeping lists for other people, making promises too quickly, laughing when I wanted to say no because laughing was easier than disappointing anyone. I remembered warmth too: a favorite book, rain on the windows, the thrill of building something that worked. My old life had not been entirely misery. That made the choice harder.\n\nThere were people I had loved, even if I couldn't yet recall their faces. There were obligations that belonged to me and others I had accepted because nobody asked whether I wanted them.\n\nThe door did not tell me which was which.\n\nI sat on the floor and cried. Not because the room was terrible, but because it was familiar enough to hurt.\n\nThe room contained evidence of a life I had made, but not a verdict on who I was.\n\nA small pile of mended clothes sat in the drawer beneath the desk. The needlework was uneven. I touched a seam with my thumb and remembered sitting at that desk one rainy evening, sewing too quickly because I had promised the shirt back by morning. I remembered the relief when nobody asked why I'd made the promise in the first place.\n\nBeside the letters was a list of repairs. Every task had a date and a box. Some had been crossed out; others had been copied again on new pages. At the bottom, in handwriting that was unmistakably mine, I had written: *Do it properly or don't do it at all.*\n\nI sank into the chair.\n\nThe sentence sounded like the voice that followed me when I fixed the bridge lanterns. I did not know whether it belonged to someone who had hurt me or to a rule I'd made to survive an impossible week. The difference mattered. I couldn't tell from a page.\n\nI opened one envelope. It contained a thank-you note for helping someone move furniture, written by a person whose name I didn't yet recognize. The note was kind. It did not say I owed them anything.\n\nThat hurt differently.\n\nI had been expecting a past built entirely from bad choices. Instead I found evidence that people had sometimes loved me without demanding labor in return. I had also found the old habit of making each kindness into another task.\n\nI put the letter back.\n\nThrough the curtain I could see a narrow strip of sky, the kind of grey that means rain is nearby. The window latch stuck when I tried to open it. My hand knew precisely how to lift the left corner and turn the catch. For a moment the ease of it made me dizzy.\n\nA memory did not have to be a command.\n\nI could remember the latch without moving back into this room. I could keep the thank-you letter without promising to be available to its author forever. I could return another day, or not.\n\nI went to the threshold and found Neri waiting on the far side of the hall, looking at a book to give me privacy.\n\n\"Can I leave it as it is?\" I asked.\n\n\"The room?\"\n\n\"Everything. I don't want someone sorting it while I'm gone.\"\n\nNeri shut the book. \"We can mark the room as private and preserve it. If you later want help cataloging your own things, you can ask.\"\n\n\"Would I have to explain what I'm keeping?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe word was wonderfully uncomplicated.\n\nI took one small notebook from the desk, not the whole pile. Then I closed the drawer and returned the key to my pocket.\n\n\"I don't want to decide my whole life today,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then don't.\"\n\nIt was almost funny, how much relief fit inside two ordinary words.\n\nAt the doorway I stopped long enough to examine the hinges. The metal was ordinary, worn by years of use. Nothing about it announced that entering would bind me to the place again.\n\n\"Will it stay here if I leave?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"The door may close between crossings. The room does not vanish because you choose not to enter it.\"\n\nI thought of the letters on the desk. I hadn't read them all. Some were addressed to me, others to people whose names still meant nothing. One envelope had been opened and resealed so many times that the corner had gone soft.\n\n\"I don't want you to sort those for me,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then I won't.\"\n\nWe wrote down what I was taking: one notebook, the little brass key, and the memory of a room that had once held both good days and bad ones. The letters could stay in place until I decided whether I wanted to read them, contact their writers, or ask for help understanding them.\n\nI stood in the corridor until the shaking in my hands eased. When I was ready, I walked back through the Threadway.\n\nWhen I returned to Lanternwell, Maui was draped over a bench with the melodramatic misery of a demigod specifically forbidden to perform a rescue. He'd brought enough food for five people and left it within my reach. A gull was stalking the bread. Maui glared at it like he'd finally found an opponent worthy of his reputation.\n\nHe rose too quickly when he saw me, nearly sending a basket of fruit rolling down the path.\n\n\"Good news,\" he announced. \"I have heroically refrained from breaking into the mysterious room, turning into a bird to peek through the window, or interrogating the ancient door about its secrets.\"\n\n\"That's quite a list.\"\n\n\"Terrible afternoon. Very little glory.\"\n\nMini Maui punched the air in triumph and immediately pointed toward the fruit. Maui looked at the banana he'd nearly crushed in his fist, then offered it to me with an embarrassed flourish.\n\n\"I was going to make a grand speech about whatever you found,\" he admitted. \"Didn't know whether you'd want one. So I brought lunch instead. Far less impressive.\"\n\n\"Much more useful.\"\n\nHe brightened. \"I knew it! The great Maui, champion of lunch.\"\n\nI took the fruit. He didn't ask me to tell him what had happened behind the door. The restraint was real; so was the loud, ridiculous man performing it.\n\n“Did you remember?” he asked.\n\n“Some of it.”\n\n“Are you going back?”\n\n“Maybe, someday. There are things I need to understand. People I may want to find. But I don't want to go back just because I know the way.”\n\nHe didn't try to tell me what to do.\n\n“I was afraid you'd think I'd become somebody else,” I admitted.\n\nHis laugh was gentle. “Jasper, I'm a shapeshifter. I would be a spectacular hypocrite.”\n\n“I'm serious.”\n\n“So am I.” He crouched beside me. “I liked you when you couldn't tell me your name. I like you now that you can tell me when I'm being an idiot. I'm not measuring you against a stranger in a room I've never seen.”\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n“What if I find out I wasn't very brave?”\n\n“Then we start with the brave thing you did today.”\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“You came back because you wanted to, not because I asked.”\n\nThe door behind us closed quietly. My key did not vanish. The choice would remain available.\n\nNeri met us before dusk with a folded paper and no attempt to ask what I had seen.\n\n“The room is recorded as a voluntary return route,” they said. “That means you may use it. It doesn't mean we can publish your memories.”\n\nMaui looked affronted. “Obviously. I'm a master of respecting secrets. I've kept at least four just this afternoon.”\n\nMini Maui shook its head.\n\nNeri explained that the crossing register recorded whether a doorway was safe to use, not what travelers thought or whom they planned to visit. I could ask for an escort without surrendering my whole history.\n\nMaui opened his mouth, ready to volunteer to carry me through the door next time. Then he stopped.\n\n“You can ask,” I said.\n\n“Fine. Would you like the great Maui's spectacularly voluntary assistance?”\n\n“Not today.”\n\nHe sighed. “A whole day of heroic restraint. Nobody sings about the hard stuff.”\n\nNeri let me decide whether to keep the paper. I did. Memory had given me facts; these people were giving me choices that survived uncertainty.\n\nWhen I asked Maui to carry the dinner basket, he grinned as if I'd entrusted him with a legendary quest. He did not pretend it was the same thing as deciding my future.\n\n\nThat night I opened the notebook I'd begun in the bakery room. On one page I wrote the things I wanted to remember: the taste of bread, the sound of rain, the satisfaction of repairing a lantern, the way the sea changed color at dusk.\n\nOn the next page I wrote the things I wanted to learn: how to sail better, how to say no without an apology, how to return to an old place without becoming an old fear.\n\nMaui looked over the list and pointed at the final blank line.\n\n“Forgot one.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“How to win an argument with a demigod.”\n\nI picked up my pencil.\n\n“I thought I'd already mastered that.”\n\n“Outrageous.”\n\nI wrote it down anyway.","word_count":1637},"maui-34":{"number":34,"title":"A Nail from the Commonwealth","content":"The new fastener looked perfect until I pulled it from the board.\n\nOrange rust coated the threads. The metal was rough where it should have been smooth, and the hole in the timber had widened enough for seawater to gather around it.\n\nMaui stared at it.\n\n\"That came from Danse. I'm going to have a very dignified conversation with him. By which I mean pointing at this rust until it apologizes.\"\n\n\"From Brasswatch,\" I corrected. \"It might have come through his workshop. That doesn't make it his fault.\"\n\n\"It was supposed to last.\"\n\n\"So was the old rope.\"\n\nFen examined the piece under a magnifying lens. We'd found it during the morning inspection at the lower landing, where Lio's skiff had begun knocking against a repair bracket. Nobody had been injured. We had taken the mooring out of service immediately.\n\nTamsin joined us with the purchase record.\n\n\"Salvage fastener,\" she read. \"Recovered from a dry storage shed in the Commonwealth. Inspected for shape and load.\"\n\n\"Not for salt,\" Fen said.\n\nTamsin closed her eyes.\n\n\"Not for salt.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms. \"Why didn't anyone check?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe winced. \"Right. That's the question. Not the accusation.\"\n\nWe carried the rusted nail to Brasswatch.\n\nDanse was teaching Renn how to test a water pump. He took one look at the fastener and put the training piece aside.\n\n\"That's not acceptable,\" he said.\n\n\"Can you tell us why?\" I asked.\n\nHe turned the nail under the light.\n\n\"Corrosion. The protective coating was already compromised. Dry use might not have exposed it quickly, but coastal spray would.\"\n\nToph arrived from the next workroom, following the sound of our conversation.\n\n\"Something's wrong with the dock?\"\n\n\"One bracket,\" Tamsin said. \"The rest is being inspected.\"\n\nToph held the fastener carefully, testing its weight. \"This isn't magic, is it?\"\n\nDanse looked offended. \"No.\"\n\n\"Good. I hate when people make bad engineering interesting by calling it a curse.\"\n\nRenn fetched the batch ledger. There were twelve nails from the same salvaged lot. Five had been used at Tideglass, three on a protected shed in Brasswatch, and four remained in storage.\n\n\"We need all of them identified,\" Danse said.\n\nMaui looked toward the door. \"I'll go tear them out.\"\n\nHis hook was already in his hand. For one horrible instant I could picture him ripping half the pier to pieces in pursuit of a problem that was holding the other half up.\n\n\"Great Maui to the rescue,\" he announced through clenched teeth. \"One hundred rusty nails, zero survivors.\"\n\n\"Five installed here,\" Danse corrected, looking at the ledger. \"Twelve in the batch.\"\n\n\"You're ruining the rhythm.\"\n\nMini Maui appeared beside the hook tattoo and drew a small diagram of a collapsing dock. The larger Maui stared at it, then at the real water below the window.\n\n\"Okay, yes,\" he muttered. \"Maybe not the whole dock.\"\n\nHe had extraordinary strength and absolutely no patience for watching a problem become a spreadsheet. The waiting made him twitch. Yet when Tamsin asked him to brace a beam while she checked the replacement, he planted himself there and held it as steadily as a mountain.\n\n\"Just so we're clear,\" he told me, \"I am still the strongest person here.\"\n\n\"Nobody disputed that.\"\n\n\"Good. Because I needed to hear it.\"\n\nHe grinned. The grin was not an apology for being wrong. The decision to wait was.\n\n\"Not all at once,\" Tamsin said. \"Some are holding things in place until we brace the structure.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nWe made a list of locations and assigned inspection teams. Fen closed the affected landing section. Tamsin designed temporary supports. Danse checked the material specification and compared replacement options. Renn contacted the supplier without accusing them of fraud; the material had been sold for general salvage, not coastal structural service.\n\nThe supplier replied that afternoon. They had no evidence the pieces had been rated for salt exposure.\n\nIt was a mistake, not a conspiracy.\n\nI could see Maui struggling with that.\n\n\"A villain would be easier,\" he admitted while we waited for the new hardware. \"You fight them. You win. Everyone gets a safe dock.\"\n\n\"Instead we have paperwork.\"\n\n\"An evil thing.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nRenn had another question before we ordered replacements.\n\n\"How do we know the next lot is actually what the label claims?\"\n\nIt was the sort of question I was embarrassed not to have asked. Danse put down the faulty fastener and looked at the supplier's old order sheet.\n\n\"We should have checked the material specification against the conditions at Tideglass,\" he admitted. \"A second invoice isn't a test.\"\n\nTamsin suggested separating the remaining salvaged stock into marked bins: dry interior use, pending verification, and unsafe. Fen wanted the rejected lot traced to its origin without claiming a faulty batch proved malicious intent. Lio wanted to know whether the temporary closure would cost the small boat crews an entire day of work.\n\n\"Not if we schedule the repairs around the safe tide window,\" Fen said. \"But they'll need notice.\"\n\nI wrote the closure times on a draft sign. Edda, who had come to deliver a route report, looked at it and pointed out that the detour described a set of steps without naming an accessible alternative. I added the upper path and asked her to test the directions on the ground before we posted them.\n\nMaui watched the growing list.\n\n\"Is there anything about this nail that doesn't involve a committee?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Danse said. \"It fails in salt water.\"\n\nToph barked a laugh. So did I.\n\nThe new order listed the actual corrosion rating, the use conditions, and a person responsible for checking the certificate before installation. We set aside money for labor rather than asking the repair crew to donate its afternoon. I wanted the change to look more impressive than a receipt. It didn't.\n\nIt would, however, keep the next person from having to pull rust out of the same board while pretending nobody could have known.\n\nThe replacement fasteners cost more. They had a suitable corrosion rating and came with a maintenance schedule. Mara agreed to cover part of the immediate cost from the repair fund; the remaining expense went to the next council meeting instead of disappearing into someone's unpaid time.\n\nThe next argument was about the invoice, not the nail.\n\nTamsin wanted tested saltwater brackets. The price made Maui whistle.\n\n“For that much I could haul the pier onto dry land every morning.”\n\n“And every evening?” Fen asked.\n\nMaui considered. “I'd sell tickets.”\n\nDanse did not laugh. “A documented failure prevents the next crew from using the same unsuitable batch.”\n\n“You're writing a report about one nail?”\n\n“I'm writing a report so the next person doesn't lose an entire landing because we were embarrassed by one nail.”\n\nNeither man liked being wrong, but they were frightened of different things. Maui feared that his strength couldn't help. Danse feared that an unrecorded mistake would be repeated.\n\nI asked Lio what closing the mooring cost his crew. He named the missed trips and the wages still owed. Fen added the next tide window. Tamsin revised the work plan rather than turning the harbor into a stage for either man's pride.\n\nMaui finally leaned toward Lio. “All right. Need somebody to lift something?”\n\n“After we clear the path,” Lio said.\n\n“Excellent. I'm very good at clearing paths.”\n\n“You're better at asking,” I told him.\n\n“That's a cruel thing to say to a legend.”\n\nHe asked anyway. The repairs cost money and time. Nobody had to pretend otherwise just because a demigod was willing to work for applause.\n\n\nWe worked in daylight. Fen supervised the lift. Lio held a support while the first bracket came free. Maui braced the timber without taking over the job. Danse and Tamsin checked the fit. Toph tested the movement through the stone foundation and reported what she could feel, without pretending she could inspect the metal's internal condition.\n\nWhen the first replacement passed, we cheered.\n\nThen we tested four more.\n\nBy sunset the mooring was usable again at a reduced load. The final load test would happen after the next tide cycle. We posted the restriction at the landing in large letters.\n\nMaui looked at the sign.\n\n\"Does everything here come with conditions?\"\n\n\"Anything worth keeping does,\" Fen said.\n\nI thought about the relationship agreement on our kitchen table. That had conditions too: honesty, choice, the ability to revise. It hadn't made our affection less real.\n\nDanse washed his hands at the work sink.\n\n\"I authorized the first batch for use,\" he said. \"I'll document where I failed to check the environment.\"\n\nTamsin looked at him.\n\n\"I approved the installation.\"\n\n\"And I carried the materials,\" Maui added.\n\nFen nodded. \"Then the report will show what each of you did. Nobody benefits if you turn the record into a competition for blame.\"\n\nRenn wrote the final note. Their handwriting was small and tidy.\n\nAt dinner, the group ate fish soup by the sheltered garden. Danse stayed for one bowl before returning to Brasswatch. Iroh sent tea from Jasmine Steps. Greg arrived late and asked whether the dock was going to make a dramatic noise again.\n\n\"Not if we can help it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked faintly disappointed.\n\nWhen everyone left, Maui held the corroded nail in his palm.\n\n\"We should keep it,\" he said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"So I remember to ask what a thing was made for, not just whether I can lift it.\"\n\nWe set it on the shelf beneath the tide chart, in a little clear jar labeled *Not for the sea.*\n\nMaui leaned toward me.\n\n\"Can I kiss you before you make another label?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me until I forgot what I'd been about to write. Later I found the pencil under the table and laughed.\n\nThe next morning Fen's report came back: the tide test had passed within the posted limits.\n\nThe jar remained where we could see it.\n\nNot every failure deserved a legend. Some deserved the right replacement part and the humility to read its specification.","word_count":1674}}};for(const story of library.series){const entries=changes[story.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of story.chapters){const e=entries[ch.id];if(!e||e.number!==ch.chapter_number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.title=e.title;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}})();
